Let (1.2)--(1.4) hold and assume that $u \in C^2(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a solution of
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{div}[\nabla f(\nabla u)] = \sum_{i=1}^n \partial_i (f_i'(\partial_i u)) = 0 \quad \text{on } \mathbb{R}^n.
\end{equation}
Suppose that we have for the exponents $p_i$
\begin{equation}
n \le 2 + \min \{p_i : i = 1, \dots, n\}.
\end{equation}
Then the Liouville property holds for equation (1.5), which means that bounded solutions must be constant.

Let the assumptions of Theorem 1.1 hold with the exception of (1.6). We will show that the hypothesis
\begin{equation}
\limsup_{|x| \rightarrow \infty} \frac{|u(x)|}{|x|^\beta} < \infty
\end{equation}
for the choice ($p_{\min} := \min \{p_i : i = 1, \dots, n\}$)
\begin{equation}
\beta := \frac{1}{p_{\min}} (p_{\min} + 2 - n)
\end{equation}
implies that the entire solution $u$ is an affine function. Now, if (1.6) is valid, we have $\beta \ge 0$, and if in addition we assume the boundedness of $u$, then (1.7) holds yielding $\nabla^2 u = 0$ which clearly implies the constancy of $u$.

In the case $n=2$ we get $\beta = 1$, which means: 
if $u \in C^2(\mathbb{R}^2)$ is an entire solution of equation (1.5) under the conditions (1.2)--(1.4), for which
\begin{equation*}
\limsup_{|x| \rightarrow \infty} |u(x)| / |x| < \infty,
\end{equation*}
then $u$ must be affine.
